SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address Before a Joint Session of the Congress on the State of the Union","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-01","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-before-joint-session-the-congress-the-state-the-union-28","claim":"Biden said the U.S. and its allies had spent months building coalitions, imposed powerful sanctions on Russia, and were providing more than $1 billion in direct assistance to Ukraine.","locator":"lines 68-77","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential address with direct statements of strategy and action.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 program and crisis-response evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable: Biden described choices he said his administration had already made and was continuing to execute, though allied and congressional cooperation mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The same speech shows the program was shaped by Russia's invasion and allied coordination, not unilateral presidential control."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.3","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address Before a Joint Session of the Congress on the State of the Union","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2023-02-07","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-before-joint-session-the-congress-the-state-the-union-29","claim":"Biden claimed that over the prior two years his administration had created 12 million jobs, passed the infrastructure law, the PACT Act, and more than 300 bipartisan laws.","locator":"lines 78-87","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential address summarizing prior-year results and implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective evidence about the 2022 governing program and its early results.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable: the claims are Biden's own summary of his administration's progress, but they depend on Congress, agencies, and broader economic conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is self-reported and does not by itself prove causation for every claimed outcome."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: President Biden's Budget Advances A Bipartisan Unity Agenda","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-28","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-president-bidens-budget-advances-bipartisan-unity-agenda","claim":"Biden's FY 2023 budget advanced his unity agenda through targeted investments in mental health, cancer, veterans, and opioids, while also aiming to cut the deficit by more than $1 trillion over the next decade.","locator":"lines 62-65 and 68-95","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary budget fact sheet with explicit priorities and funding direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 program articulation and budget translation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as the president's budget proposal, though enactment depended on Congress.","contrary_evidence":["Budget proposals are not implementation, and some priorities still needed legislative and agency follow-through."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: Biden-Harris Administration Prioritizes Effectiveness, Accountability, and Transparency in Bipartisan Infrastructure Law Implementation","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-04-29","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-biden-harris-administration-prioritizes-effectiveness-accountability-and","claim":"OMB issued initial guidance to agencies to build an effective, efficient, and equitable implementation foundation for the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, with emphasis on oversight and watchdog collaboration.","locator":"lines 62-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary implementation guidance from the White House/OMB with concrete administrative direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable through White House/OMB direction, though actual delivery depended on agencies and state/local recipients.","contrary_evidence":["The document signals setup and oversight, not completed project delivery."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: President Biden Signs Executive Order to Implement the CHIPS and Science Act of 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-president-biden-signs-executive-order-implement-the-chips-and-science-act-2022","claim":"Biden signed an executive order to implement CHIPS, establish a steering council and priorities, and move the semiconductor program toward lower costs, domestic manufacturing jobs, and more resilient supply chains.","locator":"lines 63-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential implementation order with explicit organizational structure and goals.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as a presidential action; later Commerce execution still mattered for actual awards and buildout.","contrary_evidence":["This is implementation architecture, not proof that new factories or jobs were already delivered in 2022."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: PACT Act Delivers on President Biden's Promise to America's Veterans","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-02","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-pact-act-delivers-president-bidens-promise-americas-veterans","claim":"The White House said Congress had answered Biden's call by passing the PACT Act, described as the most significant expansion of toxic-exposure veterans' benefits in more than 30 years.","locator":"lines 63-64","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary White House fact sheet connecting Biden's call to enacted legislation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 program-to-law evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Strong but not exclusive: Biden's public agenda and push were central, but the law was bipartisan and implemented by VA.","contrary_evidence":["Passage alone does not show full delivery; implementation continued after enactment."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on the Federal Student Loan Debt Relief Program and an Exchange With Reporters","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-the-federal-student-loan-debt-relief-program-and-exchange-with-reporters","claim":"Biden announced the launch of the student-debt relief application site and said the process would be simple, fast, and available online in English and Spanish.","locator":"lines 65-67","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential remarks describing the live rollout of the application process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden and his administration, though courts later blocked delivery of the relief itself.","contrary_evidence":["The relief plan soon faced litigation and did not fully deliver in 2022."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: The Biden-Harris Administration's National Security Strategy","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-the-biden-harris-administrations-national-security-strategy","claim":"The 2022 National Security Strategy said the administration would pursue a free, open, prosperous, and secure world by using all elements of national power, building the strongest possible coalition of nations, and investing in the underlying sources and tools of American power.","locator":"lines 65-66","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential strategy summary with explicit objectives and method.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 program evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as the president's strategy, though many instruments were shared across the executive branch and allies.","contrary_evidence":["The strategy sets direction; it does not itself prove successful execution."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.3","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement of Janet L. Yellen, Secretary of the Treasury, and Shalanda D. Young, Director of the Office of Management and Budget, on Budget Results for Fiscal Year 2022","publisher":"U.S. Department of the Treasury","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy1043","claim":"Treasury said the FY 2022 deficit fell by $1.4 trillion to $1.375 trillion, half of FY 2021 and $1.8 trillion below the deficit Biden inherited, while the administration said the economy had recovered the jobs lost in the pandemic and added more than 10 million jobs since Biden took office.","locator":"lines 605-610 and 615-616","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary fiscal report with concrete budget and labor-market figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 outcome evidence within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: fiscal results reflect Biden-era policy, pandemic unwind, higher revenues, and broader macro conditions, so attribution is shared rather than exclusive.","contrary_evidence":["Treasury itself credits pandemic spending unwinding and revenue effects, so the deficit change is not solely presidential action."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gross Domestic Product, Fourth Quarter and Year 2022 (Second Estimate)","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis","publication_date":"2023-02-23","url":"https://www.bea.gov/news/2023/gross-domestic-product-fourth-quarter-and-year-2022-second-estimate","claim":"BEA reported that real GDP increased 2.1 percent in 2022, with growth driven mainly by consumer spending, exports, inventory investment, and nonresidential fixed investment.","locator":"lines 78-81","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national accounts release with standard statistical methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Post-window statistic describing the 2022 year.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: the figure reflects Biden-era policy in a broader macro environment, but also inherited rebound, private demand, and Federal Reserve tightening.","contrary_evidence":["GDP growth coexisted with high inflation and was not a clean measure of presidential competence by itself."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Employment Situation - December 2022","publisher":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics","publication_date":"2023-01-06","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_01062023.pdf","claim":"BLS reported 3.5 percent unemployment in December 2022, 4.5 million payroll jobs added over 2022, and notable gains in health care, construction, and social assistance.","locator":"pages 0-2, lines 7-9, 46-49, 74-77, 84-93, 116-119","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary monthly labor-market release with direct annual totals and sector detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: labor-market recovery was strongly shaped by prior pandemic policy and macro conditions, but it is still relevant to Biden-era execution and claimed recovery.","contrary_evidence":["Participation and some sectors remained below pre-pandemic levels, and inflation remained elevated."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Estimated Budgetary Effects of H.R. 5376, the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022","publisher":"Congressional Budget Office","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/58366","claim":"CBO estimated that enacting the Inflation Reduction Act would reduce the deficit by $90 billion over 2022-2031.","locator":"lines 35-37","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary cost estimate from the congressional scorekeeper.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 legislative implementation estimate.","ruler_attribution":"Partial to strong: Biden championed the law, but the estimate concerns enacted legislation and not just presidential action.","contrary_evidence":["Cost estimates do not themselves prove implementation success or real-world macro impact."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.7","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: United States 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-united-states-2022_eeb7cbe9-en.html","claim":"The OECD said the U.S. economy rebounded strongly from the pandemic recession, aided by a large and enduring government policy response, and that the administration was reinforcing public welfare through packages investing in infrastructure and the climate transition.","locator":"lines 3731-3733","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral economic survey with explicit evaluation of policy and outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment of the Biden period.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: OECD explicitly credits policy response, but also notes the rebound was from the pandemic recession and that Ukraine war/inflation complicated the outlook.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD also said Russia's war against Ukraine and strong inflationary pressures dampened the outlook."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: United States 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-united-states-2022_eeb7cbe9-en.html","claim":"The OECD said infrastructure gaps were being addressed through the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act, and that the Inflation Reduction Act would let Medicare negotiate prices for some drugs; it also flagged governance challenges in procurement and infrastructure planning.","locator":"lines 3907-3912","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral survey with specific implementation and policy design observations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evaluation of policy translation and implementation constraints.","ruler_attribution":"Partial: the policy architecture is Biden's, but the OECD also emphasizes that execution quality depends on procurement and planning frameworks.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD warns that the drug-negotiation scope is limited and that governance challenges remain in infrastructure delivery."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.8"]}

